    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4054",
      "problem": "Consider the graph of $y = f(x)$ shown below. What is the equation of its vertical asymptote?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/63fe6fd9a6a830b4f3f446d265ae57c24bec2e5f23917c4a5dda4000c63198e8.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $x = a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y = f(x)$ if $f(x) \\to \\pm \\infty$ as $x \\to a^+$ or $x \\to a^-.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/596435060b73c6df3b05bf5f56b4650c2e77ecb70203f3d680ad9d0dddc1f027.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty.$ Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty.$\n\nSo, we can write: \n\n- $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -1^-$\n\n- $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -1^+$\n\nThese give us the same asymptote $x = -1.$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/observations/step-e4054-6c3922019e7fc7362af3.html",
